>
> I went for {srinanba} at one point, but it's not very good.
> (btw: "Pasta" in Italian refers to any kind of bread/grain, from what a 
> Milano friend tells me. It's a catch-all for the bottom of the food 
> pyramid.)
>

That's what I thought. There is just no such thing as "pasta" . 
Etymologically it's just {pesxu}. These days it's just {nanba}.
What is now known all over the world through Sbarro and similar cafes is 
probably noodles. Of just {nanba} rebranded as "pasta".

